  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Critical fire weather this weekend, with the strongest winds\n  occurring Saturday evening rather than peak afternoon heating.\n\n- Choppy lakes are expected Saturday afternoon and evening. \n\n- Cooler and more pleasant Sunday into Monday.  \n\n- Dry with seasonable temperatures next week. \n\nHigh confidence remains in a critical fire weather pattern this\nweekend as a dry Pacific trough pushes a cold front through the\nregion. Pre-frontal heating peaks on Saturday afternoon with \ntemperatures approaching 100 degrees in valleys south of I-90 and \nin southwest Montana. Lakes including Flathead Lake will become\nchoppy as the westerly winds develop by mid to late afternoon. The\ndry cold front will move through Saturday evening causing winds to\nincrease in places. There's a 60-70% chance for wind gusts\nexceeding 35 mph across the western Bitterroot Valley. \n\nIt will be much cooler on Sunday as highs will range from the 70s\nto 80s in the valleys. It will be breezy and you may need to \nbring a jacket in the higher reaches of Glacier National Park \nwhere highs may only be in the lower to middle 60s. \n\nIf you think the 60s were chilly at Logan Pass Sunday, on Monday, \nthe upper trough will pass over the region...highs may only be in\nthe upper 50s there. Lower valleys will generally be in the 70s \nand 80s...5 to 10 degrees below average for this time of year. \n\nEnsemble clusters depict a wide variety of solutions in how the\npattern will evolve next week. Overall, the temperatures will\nmoderate to seasonable levels by mid-week and there is little\nchance for precipitation.",
